  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cool air mass likely brings frost to parts of\nthe region tonight, particularly across the Poconos and \nnorthwestern New Jersey.\n\nThe cool air mass will peak today as high pressure builds more\nfirmly into the region. It will start to vacate the region\ntonight as the high shifts eastward and winds start to turn more\nsouthwesterly, but perhaps not quite quickly enough. While dew\npoints will start to rise, light winds and clear skies should\ntry to promote some radiational cooling. Undercut NBM temps most\nareas given this and included patchy frost in the normally\ncooler areas including interior southern NJ, but held the frost\nadvisory to the far north (Poconos and Sussex NJ) where air will\nprobably take longest to moisten back up, bringing the best\nchance of radiating to near freezing there. That said, wouldn't\nbe at all shocked if some colder locations further south,\nparticularly across the NJ Pine Barrens, made a run at 32\ntonight. Also wouldn't rule out some expansion of the frost\nadvisory, but confidence at this time is a bit too low for that.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Tranquil weather will prevail this week \ncontinuing into the weekend with no significant weather hazards \nanticipated. \n\nHigh pressure will be in control through much of the week \nresulting in mostly clear/sunny skies and seasonably cool to \nmild temperatures. \n\nAs high pressure shifts offshore by Wednesday, airmass \nmodification will commence. Temperatures return to more \nseasonable values on Wednesday, and likely go above normal on \nThursday with highs in the mid to upper 70s. Temperatures look \nto return to near normal again for Friday and into the weekend. \nIt should be an extended stretch of pleasant weather days with \nthe next chance for rain not arriving until Sunday at the \nearliest.",
